course: dictionary
lesson: 13458
title: Humankind, all people together
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Humankind is a noun. It means all people together, the whole human race. Think of one big group that includes every person on Earth.
explain: Humankind is uncountable, so you never write "a humankind" or "two humankinds". Say «Humankind is changing fast», not «Humankinds are changing fast».
explain: Context helps you spot its meaning. «Scientists are studying the history of humankind» is about everyone in the world, not just one family. Church life and history often use the word this way too.
explain: Check a meaning by swapping it in. Try "all people together" in «the future of humankind». It becomes «the future of all people together», and that still makes sense.
fact: Humankind means all people together; it is another name for the human race.
fact: Humankind is uncountable: we say «humankind is», not «humankinds are».
fact: «The history of humankind» means the story of all people across time.
fact: Christians believe that God created humankind.
fact: Useful phrases: all humankind, the future of humankind, for the good of humankind.
exercise: «Scientists study the history of humankind.» What does humankind mean here? | The people of one country | Only the animals on Earth | *All people in the world together | A single family in one village
exercise: Which phrase means "the future of all people"? | The path of a river | A small school in the hills | A sunny day at the beach | *The future of humankind
exercise: Which sentence is correct? | Two humankinds live on the island. | Humankinds are very clever. | A humankind has four legs. | *Humankind is working hard to protect the planet.
test: «Christians believe God created humankind.» What does humankind mean in this sentence? | A holy book | One special person | A church building | *All people together
test: Choose the correct word: "___ has learned a great deal over time." | *Humankind | A humankind | Humankinds | Two humankind
exam: Which sentence uses humankind correctly? | I saw two humankinds at the park. | *Humankind has learned a great deal over many years. | A humankind lives in that big house. | Humankinds are clever and kind.
